  "grammar": {
    "title": "Dates and on = nous",
    "intro": "For dates, French uses le + number + month, with no « of » and no -th: le 14 juillet. Only the 1st is different: le premier. When speaking, on very often replaces nous: « on fait une fête » = we're having a party. on takes the il / elle form of the verb.",
    "table": [
      [
        "English",
        "French",
        "Note"
      ],
      [
        "the 1st of May",
        "le premier mai",
        "premier, not un"
      ],
      [
        "the 14th of July",
        "le quatorze juillet",
        "just the number"
      ],
      [
        "on Monday the 3rd",
        "le lundi 3 mars",
        "day before the number"
      ],
      [
        "we celebrate",
        "on fête / nous fêtons",
        "on + il form"
      ],
      [
        "in 2026",
        "en 2026",
        "en + year"
      ]
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
        "fr": "Mon anniversaire, c'est le premier mars.",
        "en": "My birthday is on the 1st of March."
      },
      {
        "fr": "On mange ensemble ce soir ?",
        "en": "Shall we eat together tonight?"
      },
      {
        "fr": "Je suis née le 12 août 2000.",
        "en": "I was born on the 12th of August 2000."
      }
    ],
    "trap": "No capital letters for months and days: « le 14 juillet ». Ordinal numbers: add -ième (deux → deuxième, trois → troisième), except premier / première. on can also mean « people in general »: « En France, on mange tard »."
  },
